<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahmad Shah I Wali</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 1422–1436</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahmad Shah I Wali was sultan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> from 1422 to 1436. He fought Vijayanagara, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warangal">Warangal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>, and moved the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalaburagi">Gulbarga</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a>, which he rebuilt. Devoted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> saints, he was remembered as a holy man, and his tomb at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashtur">Ashtur</a> near Bidar is still venerated.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-i-wali.jpg" alt="Ahmad Shah I Wali" width="257" height="350" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ahmed_Shah_Al_Wali_Bahamani.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
